<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paleolithic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Old Stone Age, Palaeolithic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Paleolithic, or Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, is the period of human prehistory marked by the first development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, extending from their earliest known use by hominins about 3.3 million years ago to the end of the Pleistocene about 11,650 years before the present. People lived in small bands and subsisted by gathering plants, fishing, and hunting or scavenging wild animals, using knapped stone tools. From about 50,000 years ago artifacts became more varied and the first art appeared, and humans reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and finally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The term was coined by John Lubbock in 1865.</p></div>
